.docname {Telemetry Service}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Reading | One value from one room at one moment | 24.5 °C in room `r2` |
    | Batch | One building's readings sent in one request | 40 readings, `POST /telemetry/ingest` |
    | Metric | A kind of reading, handled by one plugin | `temperature`, `airQuality` |
    | Plugin | One file that declares a metric: fields, unit, limits, commands | `plugins/temperature.rs` |
    | Device kind | What a sensor physically is; one device can report several metrics | `router` reports 3 counts |
    | Bounds | The limits of one metric, as named numbers | `{"maxTemp": 28, "minTemp": 16}` |
    | Breach | A field past its limit | 30 °C with `maxTemp` 28 |
    | HMAC | A signature only the holder of a key can make | `x-signature` on every batch |
    | Device key | The key one building's devices sign with, derived from a master key | `hex(HMAC(master, "b1:3"))` |
    | Epoch | A counter per building; bumping it changes the building's key | 3 |
    | Port | What the kernel needs, in its own words | `ReadingStore` |
    | Adapter | The code that does it with a real technology | `PgReadings` |

Rust, Axum, PostgreSQL with TimescaleDB, Kafka, Redis. Directory `backend/telemetry`. Role and choices: [Telemetry architecture](../architecture/telemetry-architecture.qd). Storage: [Telemetry Storage](../design/telemetry-storage.qd).

The door for every sensor reading. It checks, stores and hands on readings, owns limits, sensors and device commands, and raises an alert for every breach.

---

## Layout

Ports and adapters around a microkernel. `tests/architecture.rs` fails the build when a rule breaks.

| Path | Holds | Must not import |
|---|---|---|
| `src/types/` | Plain shapes: `Reading`, `Sensor`, `Bounds`, `BoundSpec`, `SensorPlugin`, `DomainError` | anything else in the crate, any IO crate |
| `src/kernel/` | Use cases: `ingest`, `observations`, `occupancy`, `readings`, `thresholds`, `sensors`, `actions`, `registration`, `simulation`, `devices`, `authz`, `registry`, plus `ports.rs` | `plugins`, `adapters`, IO crates |
| `src/plugins/` | `temperature`, `air_quality`, `people_count`, `device_count`, `common` | `kernel`, `adapters`, IO crates, **another plugin** |
| `src/adapters/driven/` | `postgres/`, `redis_fanout`, `kafka_producer`, `twin_directory`, `dispatch`, `simulators`, `threshold_cache`, `building_cache` | |
| `src/adapters/driving/` | `http_api/` (`controllers`, `claims`, `exceptions`), `kafka_consumer`, `occupancy_consumer` | `driven` |
| `src/adapters/ingest_auth.rs` | Signature checks, device keys | |
| `src/lib.rs`, `src/main.rs` | Router; start-up wiring | |
| `migrations/` | `0001` to `0007`, run at start | |

IO crates the kernel may never name: `sqlx`, `redis`, `rdkafka`, `axum`, `reqwest`, `prometheus`.

### Plugins and devices

| Plugin key | Value field | Bounds (key, field, direction) | Actions | Device kind |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `temperature` | `temperature` | `maxTemp` high, `minTemp` low, both on `temperature` | `setTarget(target)`, `increase(step?)`, `decrease(step?)` | `temperature` (Thermostat) |
| `airQuality` | `indoor_aqi` | `maxCo2` high on `co2`, `maxAqi` high on `indoor_aqi` | none | `airQuality` |
| `peopleCount` | `peopleCount` | `maxPeople` high | none | `peopleCount` |
| `totalDeviceCount` | `totalDeviceCount` | none | none | `router` |
| `ratioDeviceCount` | `ratioDeviceCount` | none | none | `router` |
| `estimatedOccupancy` | `estimatedOccupancy` | none | none | `router` |

- `PluginRegistry::new` refuses two plugins with one key.
- `DeviceCatalog::new` refuses a metric with no device or with two. The service does not start.
- A plugin with bounds must be in `telemetry_schema::ALERTABLE_METRICS`, or a test fails. See [Telemetry Schema](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd).

---

## Start-up

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant M as main.rs
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        participant K as Kafka
        participant R as Redis
        M->>M: probe_exit_code: started with --health?
        M->>M: read DATABASE_URL and TELEMETRY_DEVICE_MASTER_KEY, both required
        M->>PG: pool of 10, then sqlx::migrate!
        M->>M: PluginRegistry::new(plugins::all())
        M->>M: DeviceCatalog::new(plugins::devices())
        M->>M: DeviceKeys::new(master, shared key)
        M->>M: HttpDispatch::from_json(bindings.json)
        M->>R: RedisFanout::connect
        M->>M: HttpSimulators::from_json(SIMULATORS), Simulation::new
        M->>K: KafkaEvents::connect, ensure_topics in the background
        M->>M: build Ingest, Registration, AppState
        M->>K: spawn kafka_consumer, group building-registrations
        M->>K: spawn occupancy_consumer, group estimated-occupancy
        M->>M: bind 0.0.0.0:PORT, serve telemetry::router

Any `?` in this sequence stops the process: a missing key, a short key (under 32 bytes), a failed migration, a plugin clash, a bad `SIMULATORS`. Kafka being down does **not** stop it: topics are created in the background, and produces fail after 5 s until it is back.

---

## Routes

The edge strips `/telemetry`. Three **exact** paths are open at the edge and checked here by signature: `/telemetry/ingest`, `/telemetry/collector`, `/telemetry/observations`. Any sub-path, like `/telemetry/ingest/batch`, falls into the gated block and gets `401`. The same three are listed in the Istio no-principal rule. Every other route needs the gateway pass.

| Method | Path | Who calls | Check | Does |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `POST` | `/ingest` | Devices, simulators | Body HMAC | Store a batch |
| `POST` | `/observations` | `ap-collector` | Body HMAC | Count a Wi-Fi tick, pass it to Kafka |
| `GET` | `/collector?buildingId=` | `ap-collector` | Timestamp HMAC | Routers to poll |
| `GET` | `/contracts` | `dashboard` | none | Metric catalog |
| `GET` | `/devices` | `client` | none | Device kinds |
| `GET` | `/health`, `/metrics` | Probes, Prometheus | none | |
| `GET` | `/{metric}/latest?building=&roomId=` | `client` | read | Newest reading of one room |
| `GET` | `/{metric}/entireBuilding?building=` | `client` | read | Newest reading of each room |
| `GET` | `/{metric}/dashboard?building=&roomId=&timeRange=&start=&end=&aggMode=` | `client` | read | Buckets over time |
| `GET` | `/connected-devices/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read | Building device total |
| `GET` | `/thresholds/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read | Temperature limits, building and rooms |
| `PUT` | `/thresholds/buildings/{id}` | `digital-twin` | edit | Register name, rooms, optional `maxTemperature` |
| `GET`, `PATCH` | `/thresholds/{metric}/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read, edit | Building bounds |
| `PATCH` | `/thresholds/{metric}/buildings/{id}/rooms` | `client` | edit | Bounds of many rooms, all or nothing |
| `PATCH` | `/thresholds/{metric}/buildings/{id}/rooms/{roomId}` | `client` | edit | Bounds of one room |
| `GET`, `POST` | `/sensors/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read, edit | List; apply a batch |
| `GET` | `/sensors/buildings/{id}/rooms/{roomId}` | `client` | read | Sensors of one room |
| `POST` | `/device-keys/buildings/{id}` | `client` | edit | New device key |
| `GET`, `PUT`, `DELETE` | `/simulation/buildings/{id}` | `client` | read, edit, edit | Status, start, stop |
| `POST` | `/executeAction` | `client` | edit | Send a command to a device |

### Access check

`read` and `edit` in `controllers.rs` run before any data is touched:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as controllers.rs
        participant D as TwinDirectory
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant A as kernel/authz.rs
        C->>C: GatewayClaims: header present, decodes, has sub
        C->>D: domains_of(building, raw claims)
        D->>D: cache younger than 60 s?
        D->>Tw: GET /domain/{building}, x-gateway-claims, 5 s timeout
        Tw-->>D: ["eng"]
        D->>D: cache 60 s
        C->>A: read: is_member_of(claims, "eng")<br/>edit: can_edit_domains(claims, ["eng"])
        A-->>C: allow, or 403 Not authorized for this building

| Case | Answer |
|---|---|
| No header, bad base64, no `sub` | `401` |
| `digital-twin` down, slow, non-2xx | `403`. A failed lookup is never cached |
| Caller in no owning domain | `403`, `telemetry_authz_denials_total{action}` + 1 |

`edit` uses Cedar with the shared bundle from `backend/libs/auth-policy`. A role Cedar does not know is ignored, never treated as "allow all".

### Error body

Every error is `{"type": "...", "message": "..."}`:

| `DomainError` | Status | `type` |
|---|---|---|
| `Validation` | `400` | `Validation Error` |
| `Rejected` | `422` | `Rejected Error`, plus `errors: [{ref, field, message}]` |
| `NotFound` | `404` | `Not Found Error` |
| `Forbidden` | `403` | `Forbidden Error` |
| `BadGateway` | `502` | `Bad Gateway Error` |
| `Internal` | `500` | `Internal Server Error`; the real error only in the log |

---

## Ingest a batch

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant G as Device or simulator
        participant V as ingest_auth.rs<br/>verify_signature
        participant H as controllers::ingest
        participant I as kernel::Ingest::accept
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        participant K as Kafka
        participant R as Redis
        G->>V: POST /telemetry/ingest, x-signature, body
        V->>V: read body, at most 1 MiB
        V->>V: peek buildingId, DeviceKeys::accepts
        V->>H: same bytes
        H->>H: readings must be an array
        H->>I: accept(buildingId, readings)
        I->>I: 500 or fewer? building non-empty? list non-empty?
        loop each reading
            I->>I: reading_from: type, plugin, buildingId, plugin.validate
        end
        par
            I->>PG: readings.insert, one statement, on conflict do nothing
        and
            I->>PG: thresholds.resolve, through the 60 s cache
        end
        loop each reading with bounds
            I->>I: raise_breach: breaches(plugin.bounds, bounds, payload)
            I->>K: publish_breach, queued, not awaited
        end
        I->>R: fanout.publish_telemetry, one tick on telemetry:raw
        H-->>G: 202 {"accepted":true,"readings":40}

### The signature

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        S{"x-signature header?"} -- no --> U1["401, ingest unsigned"]
        S -- yes --> B{"body 1 MiB or less?"}
        B -- no --> P["413"]
        B -- yes --> Sh{"TELEMETRY_INGEST_SECRET set<br/>and it signs the body?"}
        Sh -- yes --> Ok["pass on"]
        Sh -- no --> Id{"body has a buildingId?"}
        Id -- no --> U2["401, bad signature"]
        Id -- yes --> Ep{"building registered,<br/>epoch readable?"}
        Ep -- no --> U2
        Ep -- yes --> K["key = hex(HMAC(master, id:epoch))"]
        K --> M{"HMAC(key, body) equals header,<br/>constant-time compare?"}
        M -- no --> U2
        M -- yes --> Ok

- The signature is lowercase hex HMAC-SHA256 over the **exact bytes** sent. A body formatted again by a proxy fails.
- The epoch is read from PostgreSQL on every request, never cached. A rotated key stops working on every copy at once.
- The same golden vectors (`schemas/fixtures/internal-signature.json`) are checked by the Go services, so the scheme cannot drift on one side.

### Checking each reading

`Ingest::reading_from`, for reading number `i`:

1. Not an object: `readings[i]: must be an object.`
2. `type` missing or blank: `readings[i]: type: must be a non-empty string.`
3. No plugin with that key: `readings[i]: type: unknown sensor type: humidity.`
4. Copy the reading, drop `type`.
5. The reading names another building: `buildingId: must match the batch's building (b1).` Otherwise put the batch's `buildingId` in.
6. `plugin.validate`: required fields present and of the right kind, value read.

All errors of all readings are collected, joined, and returned as **one** `400`. Nothing is stored, nothing is published.

**Example.** A batch of 40 readings for `b1`. Reading 17 has no `roomId`; reading 30 says `"type":"humidity"`.

| Step | Result |
|---|---|
| Count | 40, under 500 |
| Readings 0 to 16, 18 to 29, 31 to 39 | valid |
| Reading 17 | error from the plugin, prefixed `readings[17]:` |
| Reading 30 | `readings[30]: type: unknown sensor type: humidity.` |
| Answer | `400 {"type":"Validation Error","message":"readings[17]: … readings[30]: …"}`. 0 stored |

### Finding a breach

`types/threshold.rs`:

1. `resolve(room, building)`: the room's bounds when there are any, else the building's. Room bounds replace building bounds; they are not merged.
2. `breaches(spec, bounds, payload)`: for each `BoundSpec` of the plugin, in order:
   - this `field` already breached? Skip: one alert per field, the first listed bound wins;
   - bound value or payload value missing? Skip;
   - `high`: breach when value > bound. `low`: breach when value < bound.
3. Each breach becomes one `AlertEvent`, with the building and room names from the registration (cached 5 min), or the ids when the names are unknown or the lookup fails.

**Example.** Building `b1` has `{"maxTemp":28}`. Room `r2` has `{"maxTemp":24,"minTemp":16}`.

| Reading | Bounds used | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `r1`, 25 °C | building: max 28 | none |
| `r2`, 25 °C | room: max 24, min 16 | `high`, threshold 24 |
| `r2`, 14 °C | room | `low`, threshold 16 |
| `r1`, air quality `co2` 1200, `indoor_aqi` 160, building has `maxCo2` 1000, `maxAqi` 150 | building | **2** alerts: one on `co2`, one on `indoor_aqi` |

### What can fail

| Failure | Result |
|---|---|
| Insert fails | `500`. Nothing published, no alert. `telemetry_ingest_persist_failures_total` + 1 |
| Bounds lookup fails | Logged as `threshold evaluation failed`. Readings stored and published, **no alerts** for that batch |
| Kafka refuses an alert | Logged, counted `telemetry_alerts_published_total{outcome="error"}`. Batch still `202` |
| Redis publish fails | Logged, counted. Batch still `202` |
| Same batch sent twice | Second insert stores nothing (unique key `building_id, metric, room_id, ts`). Alerts and the tick **are** sent again |

The tick shape and its path to the browser: [Telemetry Schema](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd).

---

## Wi-Fi ticks

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant A as ap-collector
        participant V as verify_signature
        participant O as kernel::Observations::accept
        participant S as SensorStore
        participant I as Ingest::accept
        participant K as Kafka
        A->>V: POST /telemetry/observations, signed body
        V->>O: same bytes
        O->>O: from_slice into WifiObservations, unknown fields refused
        O->>O: tick.problems(): token, signal, limits
        O->>S: by_building(buildingId)
        O->>O: room_readings(tick, sensors)
        O->>I: accept(buildingId, counts), same path as a batch
        O->>K: publish_observations: the received bytes, key buildingId
        O-->>A: 202 {"accepted":true}

`room_readings`, step by step:

1. Map each **router** sensor that sits in a room: router id → room. Outdoor routers and other sensors are left out.
2. For each connected phone on a mapped router, keep its loudest hearing: `max(signal, room)`. On equal signal the room id that sorts last wins.
3. Start every room whose router **answered** at 0.
4. Add 1 to the room of each phone's loudest hearing.
5. One `totalDeviceCount` reading per room, stamped with the tick time.
6. When the tick has `devicesPerPerson`, also `ratioDeviceCount` = count ÷ factor, **rounded up**.

**Example.** Routers `r1` in `lobby`, `r2` in `hall`. Both answered. Factor 2.5.

| Phone | Heard by | Counts in |
|---|---|---|
| A | `r1` −70, `r2` −60 | `hall` (louder) |
| B | `r1` −55 | `lobby` |
| C | `r1` −60 | `lobby` |
| D | `out` (outdoor router) | nowhere |

Readings: `totalDeviceCount` lobby 2, hall 1; `ratioDeviceCount` lobby ⌈2 ÷ 2.5⌉ = 1, hall ⌈1 ÷ 2.5⌉ = 1.

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| Unknown fields refused | The bytes go to Kafka as received; a stray `mac` would travel with them |
| Answered empty room is 0, unanswered room sends nothing | A failed router did not measure its room; 0 would look like a fresh empty room |
| Counts stored before the Kafka publish | A count that cannot be stored is `500`, and the tick is not published |
| Kafka refuses | `500`; the collector drops that tick |
| Topic `wifi-observations`, retention 10 min | A tick is stale in seconds, and it is pseudonymous |
| No state between ticks | A phone on a room boundary can switch rooms each tick |

### Room counts back from `digital-twin`

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant K as Kafka estimated-occupancy
        participant C as occupancy_consumer.rs
        participant O as kernel::Occupancy
        participant I as Ingest::accept
        K->>C: EstimatedOccupancy for one building
        C->>O: rooms to estimatedOccupancy readings
        loop each chunk of 500
            O->>I: accept(buildingId, chunk)
        end
        C->>K: commit the offset

- Group `estimated-occupancy`, starts at the earliest offset, commits by hand. After an outage the missed counts are read from the topic.
- Unreadable or refused message: dropped and committed. Store failure: not committed; the consumer reconnects after 5 s and reads it again.
- `estimatedOccupancy` has no bounds and never alerts. It is never added to `totalDeviceCount`: the two place a phone differently.

---

## Collector read and device keys

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant A as ap-collector
        participant V as verify_collector_request
        participant H as controllers::collector
        A->>V: GET /telemetry/collector?buildingId=b1<br/>x-timestamp 1761300000, x-signature
        V->>V: message = collector_message(b1, timestamp)
        V->>V: is_fresh: within 300 s of now?
        V->>V: DeviceKeys::accepts(b1, message, signature)
        V->>H: pass on
        H->>H: Sensors::for_collector: routers in rooms, by building, sorted
        H-->>A: {"buildings":[{"buildingId":"b1","routers":[…]}]}

- A GET has no body, so the building and the timestamp are signed. A captured request is useless after 300 s.
- Without `buildingId` only the shared key passes, and it reads every building.
- Each router: `sensorId`, `roomId`, and `driver`, `endpoint` only when set. Never a login.

`POST /device-keys/buildings/{id}` (edit role):

1. Bump `buildings.device_key_epoch` for the building. Not registered: `404`.
2. Derive `hex(HMAC(master, "{id}:{epoch}"))` and answer `{"buildingId", "key"}`. This is the only time the key is shown.
3. The previous key fails from the next request on.

**Example.** `b1` at epoch 2. Rotation sets epoch 3. A collector still signing with the epoch 2 key gets `401` until it is given the new key. `b2` is not affected.

---

## Building registration

Two entries, one use case: `Registration::register`.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant Tw as digital-twin
        participant K as Kafka
        participant C as kafka_consumer.rs
        participant G as Registration
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        Tw->>K: building-registration-requested {buildingId, name, rooms}
        K->>C: message, group building-registrations
        C->>C: handle: JSON? has buildingId?
        C->>G: register_from_event(buildingId, payload)
        G->>G: register: parse, name not blank, no NUL character
        G->>PG: upsert building and rooms
        G->>K: building-registration-completed, ready or failed
        C->>K: commit the offset
        Tw->>G: PUT /thresholds/buildings/b1 {name, rooms, maxTemperature}
        G->>PG: same upsert, plus building maxTemp when given

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        M["request message"] --> J{"JSON with buildingId?"}
        J -- no --> D["log, commit, no answer"]
        J -- yes --> R{"register"}
        R -- "valid, stored" --> Ready["answer ready, commit"]
        R -- "invalid: no name, NUL, bad maxTemperature" --> Failed["answer failed with the reason, commit"]
        R -- "store error" --> Retry["no answer, no commit<br/>reconnect after 5 s, read again"]
        Ready --> P{"answer published?"}
        Failed --> P
        P -- no --> Retry

- A store outage is never answered `failed`: `digital-twin` deletes a building that failed, and an outage is not the building's fault.
- NUL is checked first because PostgreSQL refuses it in text, and a store error would be retried forever.
- A room without an id is dropped; a room without a name takes its id.
- Registering twice gives the same rows.

---

## Limits

| Route | Writes | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| `PATCH …/buildings/{id}` | Building bounds of one metric | Merged into what is stored (`bounds || patch`): a key not sent keeps its value |
| `PATCH …/buildings/{id}/rooms` | `{roomId: bounds}` for many rooms | Every entry checked before any is written |
| `PATCH …/rooms/{roomId}` | One room | |

Each patch is checked by `Thresholds::checked`: every key must be a bound of that metric's plugin, every value a number. `{"maxTmp": 28}` on `temperature` is `400 maxTmp: not a bound of temperature.`

`CachedThresholds` sits in front of the store:

- `resolve` answers from a cache of up to 10 000 entries, each 60 s old at most. One tick asks PostgreSQL once, only for the rooms not cached.
- Any write for building `b1` and metric `temperature` drops **every** cached room of `b1` / `temperature`, because a building row is the fallback for all rooms.
- Another copy of the service sees the change within 60 s.

---

## Sensors batch

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as client
        participant H as controllers::apply_sensors
        participant S as kernel::Sensors::apply
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        C->>H: POST /sensors/buildings/b1 {create, update, delete}
        H->>H: edit check
        H->>S: apply(b1, body)
        S->>S: arrays? 200 items or fewer? refs and ids present?
        S->>PG: rooms of b1 from the registration, sensors of b1
        loop each create, update, delete
            S->>S: check, collect ItemError
        end
        S->>PG: one transaction
        H-->>C: 200 {"created":[{"ref":"new-1","sensorId":"6f1c…"}]}

| Item | Checks |
|---|---|
| create | `ref` unique in the batch; `name` not blank; `sensorType` a device kind from `/devices`; `roomId` null or a room of the building |
| update | `sensorId` a sensor of this building, once per batch; `name` not blank when given; `roomId` as above when given |
| delete | `sensorId` a sensor of this building, once per batch |

- More than 200 items, or a body not an object: `400`.
- Building not registered: `404`.
- Any item bad: `422 {"errors":[{"ref","field","message"}]}`, nothing written.
- The server makes the id (UUID v4). A `sensorId` sent on create is ignored.
- Positions are not stored here. They belong to `digital-twin`, under the same `sensorId`.

---

## Simulation

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as client
        participant Si as kernel::Simulation::start
        participant S1 as sensor-simulator
        participant S2 as aq-simulator
        participant S3 as ap-simulator
        C->>Si: PUT /simulation/buildings/b1
        Si->>Si: any simulator configured? building registered?
        Si->>Si: plan: each sensor to the simulator claiming its kind
        par join_all, 5 s timeout each
            Si->>S1: POST /control/start {buildingId, sensors:[temperature, peopleCount]}
        and
            Si->>S2: POST /control/start {buildingId, sensors:[airQuality]}
        and
            Si->>S3: POST /control/start {buildingId, sensors:[]}
        end
        Si-->>C: 204, or 502 when any failed

- Every simulator is told, an empty list included: empty means stop, so a removed sensor stops producing.
- Outdoor sensors and kinds no simulator claims are left out.
- No simulator configured (Kubernetes): start and stop `404`.
- Status: `running` when any simulator says so; one that does not answer counts as not running.
- Sensors edited during a run are simulated from the next start.

Body shape: [Telemetry Schema, fixture-only shapes](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd).

---

## Device commands

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as client
        participant H as controllers::execute_action
        participant A as kernel::Actions::execute
        participant D as HttpDispatch
        participant PG as PostgreSQL
        participant Dev as Device
        C->>H: POST /executeAction {actionData:{metric, buildingId, sensorId, action, arguments}}
        H->>H: fields non-empty, edit check
        H->>A: execute(command)
        A->>A: plugin knows the action? arguments match its parameters?
        A->>D: dispatch(command)
        D->>PG: driver, endpoint of (buildingId, sensorId)
        D->>D: binding = bindings.json[driver][action]
        D->>D: rename arguments, check URL is http or https
        D->>Dev: POST endpoint + path, 5 s timeout
        Dev-->>D: 2xx
        H-->>C: 200 {"accepted":true,"action":"setTarget"}

**Example.** `setTarget` with `{"target": 21}` on a sensor with driver `tp-simulator` and endpoint `http://sensor-simulator:3000`. `bindings.json` maps `target` to `value`, so the device gets `POST http://sensor-simulator:3000/control/receive` with `{"value": 21}`.

| Case | Answer |
|---|---|
| Unknown metric or action | `404` |
| Missing or wrong argument | `400` |
| Sensor has no driver or endpoint, or no binding | `404` `no binding for sensor …` |
| Endpoint not http(s) | `404`, logged |
| Device answers non-2xx, or is unreachable | `502` |

The sensor is found by `(buildingId, sensorId)`, never by room, so an outdoor or moved sensor can still be reached. More: [Sensor Actions](../design/sensor-actions.qd).

---

## Reads

| Route | Answer |
|---|---|
| `latest` | One reading; `roomId` missing is `400`; no data is `404` |
| `entireBuilding` | `{"data": [reading per room]}` |
| `dashboard` | `{"data": [{"timestamp","value"}]}` |
| `connected-devices` | `{buildingId, totalDeviceCount, timestamp}`; never reported is `404` |

A reading is answered **flat**: `building`, `roomId`, `timestamp`, `value`, plus every plugin field at the top level. The same names as the live tick, so a page that loads history and then live data handles one shape.

`dashboard` query:

| `timeRange` | Window | Bucket |
|---|---|---|
| missing or `1D` | last 24 h | 1 hour |
| `1W` | last 7 days | 1 day |
| `1M` | last 30 days | 1 day |
| `custom` | `start` to `end` (end defaults to now); no `start` is `400` | 1 day |

`aggMode`: `sum`, `min`, `max`, anything else `avg`.

**Example, connected devices.** At 10:00:00 `lobby` reports 12, `hall` 5. `office` last reported at 09:40 (its router was removed). Answer: `{"totalDeviceCount": 17, "timestamp": <10:00:00>}`. `office` is left out: only rooms at the newest report count.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `DATABASE_URL` | required | PostgreSQL with TimescaleDB |
| `TELEMETRY_DEVICE_MASTER_KEY` | required, 32 bytes or more | Derives every device key |
| `TELEMETRY_INGEST_SECRET` | unset | Shared key that signs for any building. Local and simulators only |
| `REDIS_URL` | `redis://redis:6379` | Tick fan-out |
| `KAFKA_BROKERS` | `kafka:9092` | Registration, alerts, Wi-Fi, room counts |
| `DIGITAL_TWIN_URL` | `http://digital-twin:3000` | Ownership lookup |
| `SIMULATORS` | unset | JSON `{name: {url, kinds}}`. Compose gives `temperature`, `peopleCount` to `sensor-simulator`, `airQuality` to `aq-simulator`, `router` to `ap-simulator` |
| `PORT` | `3000` | HTTP port |

---

## What it stores

| Table | Holds | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `readings` | `building_id, room_id, metric, ts, value, payload` | Hypertable. Unique on `building_id, metric, room_id, ts`. Compressed after 7 days, dropped after 14 |
| `readings_hourly` | Hourly summaries | Continuous aggregate, refreshed every hour over the last 3 days |
| `thresholds` | `building_id, room_id, metric, bounds` | `room_id` null = building row |
| `sensors` | `sensor_id` (key), `building_id, room_id, name, sensor_type, driver, endpoint` | `room_id` null = outdoors |
| `buildings`, `building_rooms` | Registration: names and rooms; `device_key_epoch` | |

Kafka topics it creates: `building-registration-requested`, `building-registration-completed`, `alerts`, `wifi-observations` (10 min).

---

## Metrics

| Name | Counts |
|---|---|
| `telemetry_ingest_total{metric, outcome}` | Readings `accepted`, batches `invalid`, `unsigned`, `bad_signature` |
| `telemetry_ingest_persist_failures_total`, `…_persist_duration_seconds` | Insert failures and time |
| `telemetry_threshold_breaches_total{metric, direction}` | Breaches found |
| `telemetry_alerts_published_total{topic, outcome}` | Kafka answers to alerts |
| `telemetry_published_total{outcome}` | Redis publishes |
| `telemetry_registration_total{outcome}`, `…_duration_seconds` | `ready`, `failed`, `unacknowledged` |
| `telemetry_occupancy_ticks_total` | Room-count messages |
| `telemetry_query_duration_seconds{query}` | Read times |
| `telemetry_authz_denials_total{action}` | `read`, `edit` denials |
| `telemetry_http_requests_total`, `…_error_requests_total`, `…_duration_seconds` | Every route |
| `telemetry_db_pool_connections` | Pool use |

---

## Tests

```bash
just test telemetry               # unit, kernel against fakes, no database
just test telemetry-integration   # tests/*.rs against throwaway TimescaleDB, Redis, Kafka
```

| Where | Checks |
|---|---|
| `src/kernel/*.rs` | Every rule above against in-memory fakes: all-or-nothing batch, 500 cap, every breach its own alert, a failed name lookup still alerts by id, Wi-Fi counting cases, sensors batch errors, simulation plans, actions |
| `src/adapters/ingest_auth.rs` | Golden HMAC vectors shared with Go; collector request fixture; key per building; rotation revokes; shared key only when set; short keys refused |
| `src/adapters/driven/*.rs` | Threshold cache: one query per tick, invalidation per building and metric, capacity; twin lookup cache and timeout; dispatch renames; simulator bodies match the fixture |
| `tests/api.rs` | HTTP: `/contracts` equals `fixtures/metric-contract.json`; every `ingest-batch.json` case and rejection |
| `tests/persistence.rs` | PostgreSQL adapters |
| `tests/fanout.rs` | A tick reaches `telemetry:raw` |
| `tests/alerts.rs` | A breach reaches `alerts` keyed by building and room, even before anyone subscribes |
| `tests/registration.rs` | Ready answer; repeat converges; failed with reason; bad message does not stop the consumer; request sent before start is answered; unpublished answer is read again |
| `tests/cedar_conformance.rs` | Cedar decisions match the shared golden cases |
| `tests/architecture.rs` | The layering rules in the table above |

---

## Gaps

- **One alert per breaching reading.** A room that stays at 31 °C sends an alert with every reading; `notification` mutes repeats for 5 min.
- **No "back within limits" message.**
- **A repeated batch alerts again.** The insert drops duplicates, but breaches and the tick are sent a second time.
- **Ingest replay.** A captured, signed batch stays valid; only the collector GET has a time limit.
